WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:03.513
(upbeat music)

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.960
- Families, friends,
esteemed faculty and staff,

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:20.240
welcome to Yale College Class Day.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:24.110
And most importantly,
to the Class of 2021,

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:27.480
on behalf of me, Skye,
Steven, Jin, and Stanley,

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.200
we just want to say congratulations.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.220
For over 100 years,
Yalies have come together

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:36.310
and celebrated our graduating class

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:39.000
one last time on Class Day.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.470
I don't need to tell you
that this year's ceremony,

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:42.880
much like our senior year,

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.194
looks a little different
from its predecessors,

00:00:45.194 --> 00:00:46.960
but in today's festivities,

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:48.830
we hope to replicate and renovate

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:51.780
many of Yale's most cherished traditions.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:54.350
We'll start today off
with a series of speeches

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:56.570
from graduates and alumni.

00:00:56.570 --> 00:01:00.020
Vy Tran will open our
ceremony with the Ivy Ode.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:03.080
Then we'll hear from acclaimed
songwriter Bobby Lopez,

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:05.990
our Senior Class Council,
and a comedic reflection

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:08.930
by Matt Nadel and Teava Torres de Sa.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:11.210
To cap off the day and our time at Yale,

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:13.390
Alec Zbornak will deliver a reflection

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:16.480
on these last four years
we've shared together.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:18.770
But before we begin, I
wanted to take a moment

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:20.380
to walk you through some of the items

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.350
you received yesterday.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.410
The Class Anthology,
produced by Skye Ward,

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:26.800
details our time together

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.610
in a series of essays and works of art.

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:31.210
The Churchwarden Pipe,

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:33.990
a tradition that dates back to the 1860s,

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:35.380
used to symbolize the end

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:37.220
of a carefree college life

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:40.280
as successive generations
of Yalies would form circles

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:43.230
and crush the pipes into the earth.

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:45.550
The sprig of ivy ties us back to

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:47.990
the ceremonial planting of the Class Ivy,

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:50.818
a tradition that began way back in 1852

00:01:50.818 --> 00:01:54.220
to symbolize the abiding
legacy of our contributions

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:57.540
to the college, even
long after we graduate.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:00.090
And lastly, the Yale handkerchief,

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:01.610
which Skye will tell you more about

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:03.673
at the end of our ceremony here today.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:08.060
Yale has changed a lot since
these traditions first began.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:10.260
It's even changed in the few short years

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.230
that we've been a part of it,

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:13.530
but there are some parts of it

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:16.220
that will always remain constant.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:19.430
Here to remind us of this
enduring spirit of Yale

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:22.250
is Vy Tran, a senior in Branford College

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:26.083
studying Ethnicity, Race, and
Migration, with our Ivy Ode.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:29.580
- There's a specific feeling
of coming back to campus

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.597
after being gone for awhile.

00:02:31.597 --> 00:02:33.720
Sometimes it's been a long Uber ride

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.660
from Hartford or LaGuardia or JFK.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:38.020
Sometimes it's an Uber ride

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.683
after a train ride into Union Station.

00:02:40.683 --> 00:02:43.460
Sometimes it's in a car
your family's driving in

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:45.670
that you've known your entire life.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:47.420
Sometimes it's in the
car you're driving in

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:49.110
with your friends, coming back from a trip

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:50.560
you're still trying to revel in

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:52.610
and your body is itching to get up

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:54.510
because you've been sitting for hours.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:57.780
And pulling through Chapel or York or Elm,

00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:58.800
you know the streets,

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.683
but they also feel a little
bit different this time.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:06.110
It's the same feeling of
walking into your childhood home

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:08.520
after six months in New Haven and swearing

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:11.070
that your head is a tiny
bit closer to the ceiling.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.440
It's the same feeling as
hugging your family again

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:17.164
and wanting to show them that
you're a little bit different

00:03:17.164 --> 00:03:19.664
but that you're also the same.

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:23.510
There's a specific feeling
of hearing a voice you love

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:25.500
pop up in your common room.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:29.390
It's Thursday, and you hear
your suite door open and close

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:30.380
and you think it's your suitemate

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:31.590
just coming back from the bathroom,

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:33.830
but it's actually someone else.

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:36.000
There's a specific joy at hearing a voice

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.400
that isn't coming out of
your computer speakers.

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:42.810
It's the same feeling as hearing
one of your favorite songs

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:44.020
start to play at a party.

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:45.180
(Remember those?)

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.900
Or on the radio in the car,
or on your friend's phone

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:49.330
that's connected to the Bluetooth speaker

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:50.730
in the living room.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:53.487
And as many times as I've
heard Carrie Underwood's

00:03:53.487 --> 00:03:56.820
"Before He Cheats" or Sage
the Gemini's "Gas Pedal,"

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:58.543
the songs never don't hit.

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:03.070
It's the same feeling as
hearing that one of your friends

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:06.150
got that job they were excited
for and you are so excited

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:08.100
for everything that's coming their way.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:11.610
There's a specific feeling of seeing

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:13.740
one of your favorite
people on Cross Campus,

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:15.095
looking cute and all.

00:04:15.095 --> 00:04:18.190
You see someone you're pretty
sure is who you think it is,

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:19.700
but especially now with masks,

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:21.674
I really don't know if
we're on the "hi" level

00:04:21.674 --> 00:04:23.910
or if we're on the walk-past-each-other-

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:27.030
and-act-like-we're-looking-at-
something-over-there level.

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:30.560
But sometimes it really is
someone you know and love

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:33.724
and can be excited for, unabashedly.

00:04:33.724 --> 00:04:35.500
And they're excited to see you too.

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:39.700
It's the same feeling as
seeing red chat pop up

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:42.376
in your Zoom because someone has DM-ed you.

00:04:42.376 --> 00:04:45.290
And it's the feeling of
watching your friends smile

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:47.270
at your message in response,

00:04:47.270 --> 00:04:48.980
or both of you covering your faces

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:51.210
and turning your cameras
off because you're laughing

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:53.123
at the wrong thing at the wrong time.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:57.810
It's the same feeling as
losing your person at a party

00:04:57.810 --> 00:04:59.620
or a weird Yale event
that you're much more

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:02.237
socially anxious about than
you're willing to admit,

00:05:02.237 --> 00:05:04.367
but then you turn around and you see them

00:05:04.367 --> 00:05:06.163
and they were looking for you too.

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:10.300
A while ago, I was coming
back from a retreat

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:11.830
with a wonderful group of people.

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:14.077
And on their way back to
New Haven, someone was like,

00:05:14.077 --> 00:05:16.110
"What if X person had come?

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:18.087
Do you think that would
have made any difference?"

00:05:18.087 --> 00:05:20.600
And then someone was like, "Of course.

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:21.887
They always make a difference.

00:05:21.887 --> 00:05:23.470
Everyone makes a difference."

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:24.710
And I think about that a lot,

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:26.390
because especially in a place like Yale,

00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:29.020
it's so easy to think
that you could go missing

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:31.910
for a few days and no one would notice.

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:33.970
And every other day for
the past four years,

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:36.058
I've thought about disappearing
to go live on a farm

00:05:36.058 --> 00:05:39.168
and paint and read books
and sit with goats.

00:05:39.168 --> 00:05:41.772
But if there's anything
I've learned at Yale,

00:05:41.772 --> 00:05:46.283
it's that I am only a sum of
everyone that I've ever loved.

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:49.260
No one here knows what they're doing

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.713
or who they are or what they want in life.

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:56.167
The things that we think are worth it

00:05:56.167 --> 00:05:58.970
and the things that we're
taught will satisfy us

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:01.350
and the job and the salary
and all these other things

00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:03.591
that we think Yale is supposed to be for

00:06:03.591 --> 00:06:05.750
don't make Yale Yale.

00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:07.580
In reality, the things that make Yale Yale

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:10.530
are the people that we love
and the ways that we love them.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:13.720
We look forward to the
little things with them.

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:16.320
We look forward to seeing
if there's bacon at brunch...

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:19.700
if your house favorite will
win "The Bachelor"... (They don't.)

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:21.030
If that thing you ordered has finally

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:22.750
come into the packaging center...

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:24.840
and if it's finally gonna
be nice enough this weekend

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:26.330
to go to the farmer's market in Wooster

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:29.190
with your friends like you
always say you want to.

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:31.080
And in truth, life a week from now

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:34.113
could be exactly the same
or drastically different.

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:37.640
And while we don't always
know what we're doing,

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:39.468
we do know what we feel.

00:06:39.468 --> 00:06:44.380
We feel happy and sad and
stressed and exhausted

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:47.740
and tired of the snow
and tired of the cold.

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:51.456
We feel joy and pride
and love and loneliness

00:06:51.456 --> 00:06:53.346
and that we're growing up too fast

00:06:53.346 --> 00:06:56.053
and there's no way to stop this train.

00:06:56.053 --> 00:06:58.700
But if you look outside,

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:01.323
you'll see that the view is beautiful.

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:04.980
There's a specific feeling

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:08.103
of leaving campus after
being here for awhile.

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:12.458
And when I next see Elm
Street, I will feel older.

00:07:12.458 --> 00:07:15.407
There's that weird conversation
starter that's like,

00:07:15.407 --> 00:07:17.440
"If you could say anything
to your younger self,

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:19.070
what would you say?"

00:07:19.070 --> 00:07:21.657
And I think I would say "I'm so proud.

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:25.100
We've come so far and done so much

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:27.750
and loved so widely and so deeply,

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:29.463
and there's even more to come."

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:33.650
It's the same feeling as
packing up your suitcase

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:37.260
for the end of the semester,
or the end of Yale now,

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:39.373
and realizing that you're
not the same person you were

00:07:39.373 --> 00:07:41.053
when you came here freshman year.

00:07:42.474 --> 00:07:45.330
It's the same feeling as
taking down all your photos

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:46.890
and all your posters off your wall

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:49.147
and thinking, "I'm gonna miss this place."

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:54.200
There's a specific feeling

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:57.930
of leaving campus after
being here for awhile,

00:07:57.930 --> 00:08:01.133
and I think it's called
growing up and leaving home.

00:08:03.470 --> 00:08:05.620
And I wouldn't want to
do it anywhere else.

00:08:06.733 --> 00:08:10.000
Much love to the Class of 2021.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:11.563
Love y'all. We did it!

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:14.560
- We are delighted to
announce our renowned

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:19.520
Class Day speaker, Robert Lopez,
Yale College Class of 1997.

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:22.730
Lopez is a two-time Emmy, Grammy,

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:26.010
Oscar, and Tony award-winning songwriter.

00:08:26.010 --> 00:08:28.830
After graduating from Yale College in 1997

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:30.500
with a bachelor's degree in English,

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:33.770
he joined the BMI
Musical Theatre Workshop,

00:08:33.770 --> 00:08:36.370
where he met future
collaborators Jeff Marx,

00:08:36.370 --> 00:08:38.303
with whom he co-created the multiple

00:08:38.303 --> 00:08:41.816
Tony award-winning "Avenue
Q," and Kristen Anderson,

00:08:41.816 --> 00:08:45.550
now Kristen Anderson-Lopez,
his wife and co-writer.

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:47.280
The couples' partnership
produced the songs

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:50.645
from "Frozen," "Frozen II,"
and "Marvel's WandaVision,"

00:08:50.645 --> 00:08:53.191
as well as the song
"Remember Me" from "Coco."

00:08:53.191 --> 00:08:55.191
Lopez is also the co-creator,

00:08:55.191 --> 00:08:58.795
with Matt Stone and Trey
Parker of "South Park" fame,

00:08:58.795 --> 00:09:00.140
of "The Book of Mormon,"

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:04.370
which debuted in 2011
and won nine Tony Awards.

00:09:04.370 --> 00:09:06.570
He shared two Emmy Awards for his music

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:08.960
on Nickelodeon's "The
Wonder Pets" as well as

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:11.084
Emmy nominations for songs in "Scrubs,"

00:09:11.084 --> 00:09:14.370
the 87th Academy Awards ceremony,

00:09:14.370 --> 00:09:16.930
and the mockumentary "The Comedians."

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:19.357
His work has been featured
on the television shows

00:09:19.357 --> 00:09:22.820
"South Park," "The Simpsons,"
and "Phineas &amp; Ferb."

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:25.660
Lopez is one of only 15 artists to win

00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:28.950
all four major entertainment
industry awards,

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:31.560
Emmy, Grammy, Oscar, and Tony.

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:35.107
He was also the youngest
to do so, at age 39 in 2014,

00:09:35.107 --> 00:09:38.100
and the first to win
all four a second time.

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:39.810
He's been an amazing ambassador for

00:09:39.810 --> 00:09:41.970
the artistic talents and activities

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:44.530
that are such a big part
of the Yale experience.

00:09:44.530 --> 00:09:48.600
Without further ado, please
help me welcome Robert Lopez.

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:51.210
- Hello, congratulations,
and happy Class Day,

00:09:51.210 --> 00:09:53.680
Yale College Class of 2021.

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:55.476
I want to thank President Salovey

00:09:55.476 --> 00:09:58.051
and everyone at Yale for the
honor of speaking to you today.

00:09:58.051 --> 00:10:00.480
Actually, this is gonna
be taped over many days,

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:01.890
and I have a lot of these shirts,

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:03.560
so I think that we will be fine

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:06.410
with the illusion of continuity.

00:10:06.410 --> 00:10:09.520
So my Class Day in 1997, it rained,

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:12.120
and we all sat in chairs
for hours with umbrellas,

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:14.350
which is something I've never done since.

00:10:14.350 --> 00:10:16.610
I hope it doesn't rain
today, but if it does,

00:10:16.610 --> 00:10:18.020
please take my advice and don't

00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:19.540
be talked into sitting outside.

00:10:19.540 --> 00:10:22.423
Even with an umbrella,
you will still get wet.

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:25.160
My name is Robert Lopez.

00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:26.800
Everyone calls me Bobby.

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:29.490
I'm a songwriter and I'm here
because the administration

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:31.319
and the Class Day Committee now prefers

00:10:31.319 --> 00:10:34.490
musical theater
professionals to the typical

00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:37.410
intellectual, historian,
politician, writer,

00:10:37.410 --> 00:10:39.380
or entrepreneur they usually get.

00:10:39.380 --> 00:10:41.828
And well they should, because
at this crossroads of history

00:10:41.828 --> 00:10:43.450
a little musical theater

00:10:43.450 --> 00:10:46.230
might be just the thing for all of us.

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:47.970
And they hinted, not so subtly,

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:50.350
that I work some songs into my remarks.

00:10:50.350 --> 00:10:52.087
And for the majority of you who are like

00:10:52.087 --> 00:10:55.410
"Who the eff is this guy," here is a hint.

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:57.170
This is a rewrite of one of my songs

00:10:57.170 --> 00:10:59.660
to forever musicalize the
moment President Salovey

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:01.143
asked me to do this.

00:11:02.890 --> 00:11:06.330
♪ Do you want to speak on Class Day ♪

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:09.430
♪ I think it would be fun ♪

00:11:09.430 --> 00:11:11.531
♪ I know it's not your super power ♪

00:11:11.531 --> 00:11:16.196
♪ To talk for half an hour
in front of everyone ♪

00:11:16.196 --> 00:11:18.712
♪ But it would be exciting ♪

00:11:18.712 --> 00:11:23.712
♪ And just think of all the
lessons you'd like to teach ♪

00:11:24.717 --> 00:11:28.467
♪ Yes, I guess I'll speak on Class Day ♪

00:11:28.467 --> 00:11:33.244
♪ And I guess I'll write this Class Day ♪

00:11:33.244 --> 00:11:35.244
♪ Song ♪

00:11:40.040 --> 00:11:41.530
Today is Class Day.

00:11:41.530 --> 00:11:44.575
It's a celebration of the four
years you've spent at Yale.

00:11:44.575 --> 00:11:48.300
New Haven has changed a lot
since my Class Day 24 years ago.

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:49.576
Mostly it's better.

00:11:49.576 --> 00:11:52.370
Really, the only thing I
miss is the Yankee Doodle,

00:11:52.370 --> 00:11:55.150
an old-fashioned lunch counter
that served magical burgers

00:11:55.150 --> 00:11:57.135
and fried butter donuts.

00:11:57.135 --> 00:11:59.094
God, that place was great.

00:11:59.094 --> 00:12:02.100
I'd go in the early, early
morning for breakfast sandwiches

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:03.800
after pulling an all-nighter.

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:05.610
The only time I ever got to hang out

00:12:05.610 --> 00:12:07.160
with people from the crew team.

00:12:08.178 --> 00:12:11.550
I'll never forget there was
this one all-nighter I pulled.

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:15.259
I was writing some paper
and I left my computer on.

00:12:15.259 --> 00:12:17.490
I used to really go through despair

00:12:17.490 --> 00:12:18.640
while writing long papers

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:21.890
and I needed food to sustain my spirits.

00:12:21.890 --> 00:12:24.727
So I came back to my room with
a soda and a bag of chips.

00:12:24.727 --> 00:12:26.498
And I sat back down at my desk

00:12:26.498 --> 00:12:29.168
and where my paper had left off,

00:12:29.168 --> 00:12:31.550
there were now like a
bunch of random letters

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:35.527
like X-D-F-G-K-L semicolon slash slash.

00:12:35.527 --> 00:12:37.739
I thought, "That's weird."

00:12:37.739 --> 00:12:39.920
And then I noticed that some tissues

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:42.123
from a box on my desk had been shredded.

00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:46.850
Then I looked at my window
and there was some loose dirt

00:12:46.850 --> 00:12:48.930
and an acorn there.

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:51.870
And then right on the
ledge of the open window,

00:12:51.870 --> 00:12:55.467
a squirrel sat there,
intensely looking back at me.

00:12:55.467 --> 00:12:57.957
I screamed a bloodcurdling sound

00:12:57.957 --> 00:13:00.110
that probably woke up the entire building

00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:02.810
and the squirrel jumped out
the window onto a tree branch

00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:03.763
and never returned.

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:07.810
Honestly, I thought about this a lot

00:13:07.810 --> 00:13:10.160
and I think it was a Disney moment

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:14.420
that I was too high-strung at
the time to really appreciate.

00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:18.010
I mean, I was stuck on a
paper and a freaking squirrel

00:13:18.010 --> 00:13:21.190
came in and typed
something on my keyboard,

00:13:21.190 --> 00:13:23.490
and I just screamed and
deleted what he typed.

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:25.860
I mean, can you imagine the song

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:27.550
that the squirrel would
eventually have sung

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:29.623
if I'd only thanked him and let him stay?

00:13:31.230 --> 00:13:33.880
Anyway, I think the gist of
what I want to talk about today

00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:36.203
is accepting help from
the squirrel friends

00:13:36.203 --> 00:13:38.690
that the universe provides,

00:13:38.690 --> 00:13:41.323
accepting collaboration into your life.

00:13:43.770 --> 00:13:45.090
The week before my Class Day,

00:13:45.090 --> 00:13:46.850
we had something called Senior Week.

00:13:46.850 --> 00:13:48.980
I'm not sure if you guys were
able to have that this year,

00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:51.960
but back in my day, you were
supposed to take a fun trip

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:53.921
with your best friends
to like Myrtle Beach

00:13:53.921 --> 00:13:56.280
and party all week.

00:13:56.280 --> 00:13:58.720
I was in the acapella
group The Spizzwinks,

00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:01.410
and my two best friends
from my class and the Winks,

00:14:01.410 --> 00:14:03.340
John and Brian, were sort of late

00:14:03.340 --> 00:14:05.180
to figure out what we were gonna do.

00:14:05.180 --> 00:14:07.380
And we ended up going to Maine,

00:14:07.380 --> 00:14:11.720
which in May that year was
really cold and really damp.

00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:14.030
The cabin we rented had
a sign bearing its name,

00:14:14.030 --> 00:14:16.230
which was It'll Do.

00:14:16.230 --> 00:14:20.070
And as we sat and froze
and did jigsaw puzzles,

00:14:20.070 --> 00:14:22.010
the whole thing felt
like a bit of a metaphor

00:14:22.010 --> 00:14:24.423
for what we thought life
was about to serve us.

00:14:25.288 --> 00:14:27.560
I think we were afraid
we were going to graduate

00:14:27.560 --> 00:14:29.629
and go our separate ways
and forget each other,

00:14:29.629 --> 00:14:32.980
and that the real world
was gonna be like It'll Do

00:14:32.980 --> 00:14:36.283
in May '97: cold, damp, and depressing.

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:39.430
I think we were afraid we'd be lonely,

00:14:39.430 --> 00:14:41.680
that it was about to be
me against the world.

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:45.250
First of all, I could not have
gotten through this pandemic

00:14:45.250 --> 00:14:47.124
without John and Brian.

00:14:47.124 --> 00:14:50.080
Every Tuesday for the
last year we got on Zoom

00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:52.360
and had a scotch and
helped each other deal with

00:14:52.360 --> 00:14:53.853
whatever we had to deal with.

00:14:54.740 --> 00:14:56.290
We've been to each other's weddings.

00:14:56.290 --> 00:14:58.520
We've watched our kids be born.

00:14:58.520 --> 00:15:00.090
I mean, a lot of my Yale friends and I

00:15:00.090 --> 00:15:03.110
have stuck together and
worked together too.

00:15:03.110 --> 00:15:07.210
And the real world may be
tough, but it was not lonely.

00:15:07.210 --> 00:15:09.690
Sometimes school, with grades and tests,

00:15:09.690 --> 00:15:12.840
makes you feel like work
is something you do alone.

00:15:12.840 --> 00:15:14.820
But my experience is
that work can't be done

00:15:14.820 --> 00:15:17.030
without collaborators.

00:15:17.030 --> 00:15:19.690
I'm gonna talk out of
my butt for a moment.

00:15:19.690 --> 00:15:22.230
A great surgeon has a
team of doctors and nurses

00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:24.510
in the operating room with them.

00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:26.590
A CEO has to manage vice presidents

00:15:26.590 --> 00:15:28.840
and satisfy the board of directors.

00:15:28.840 --> 00:15:31.650
A professor works within their department.

00:15:31.650 --> 00:15:34.270
A great novelist allows a
trusted circle of friends

00:15:34.270 --> 00:15:36.830
and ultimately an editor
to read early drafts

00:15:36.830 --> 00:15:39.273
and give them necessary feedback.

00:15:39.273 --> 00:15:41.160
You get the idea.

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:43.682
I don't think there is
any field you can go into

00:15:43.682 --> 00:15:46.673
in which you can succeed
without working with others.

00:15:48.680 --> 00:15:51.560
You guys have had
collaborators your whole life

00:15:51.560 --> 00:15:52.920
without knowing it.

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:55.099
Your first collaborators were your family.

00:15:55.099 --> 00:15:56.266
When you were a baby,

00:15:56.266 --> 00:15:59.360
you were totally
dependent on your parents,

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:02.280
but without knowing it,
you were teaching them too.

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:04.600
Testing them through
your utter dependence,

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:06.620
forcing them to learn responsibilities

00:16:06.620 --> 00:16:08.370
and solve problems and gain skills

00:16:08.370 --> 00:16:10.212
they never would have learned
without a crying baby.

00:16:10.212 --> 00:16:13.920
Helping them to level up to
be able to parent a toddler,

00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:16.707
then a child, then a tween, then a teen,

00:16:16.707 --> 00:16:19.398
and now finally a 20-something.

00:16:19.398 --> 00:16:22.780
You and your family worked
together to raise you,

00:16:22.780 --> 00:16:25.770
to get you into Yale
and get you out of Yale.

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:28.330
They are incredible collaborators and

00:16:28.330 --> 00:16:31.690
they deserve credit for you
as much as you do for them.

00:16:31.690 --> 00:16:33.640
You guys should thank each other today.

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:37.910
Your classmates here at
Yale are collaborators too.

00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:39.850
You've worked in the
residential colleges together,

00:16:39.850 --> 00:16:42.030
making life better for each other.

00:16:42.030 --> 00:16:43.670
You've worked together to plan events

00:16:43.670 --> 00:16:46.490
like parties and dances and protests.

00:16:46.490 --> 00:16:48.400
You've worked together on sports teams.

00:16:48.400 --> 00:16:50.200
I didn't do that at Yale.

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:52.396
You've worked together in
acapella groups and theater,

00:16:52.396 --> 00:16:54.220
did both of those.

00:16:54.220 --> 00:16:57.410
In newspapers, comedy
groups, political clubs,

00:16:57.410 --> 00:17:01.400
ethnic, religious, and racial
identity forums, LGBTQ+,

00:17:01.400 --> 00:17:03.360
even secret societies.

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:06.580
All of these things are
examples of collaboration.

00:17:06.580 --> 00:17:08.680
You've been practicing it this whole time.

00:17:09.590 --> 00:17:11.020
And when I was preparing for the speech,

00:17:11.020 --> 00:17:12.380
I met with a faculty member

00:17:12.380 --> 00:17:14.730
and the student committee for Class Day

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:17.341
and I realized another thing
you've been collaborating on.

00:17:17.341 --> 00:17:21.515
Re-imagining Yale within
the realities of COVID.

00:17:21.515 --> 00:17:23.024
You guys know much more about

00:17:23.024 --> 00:17:25.500
what that's been like than I do.

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:26.570
And from what I've heard,

00:17:26.570 --> 00:17:29.090
it's been very difficult and stressful,

00:17:29.090 --> 00:17:32.220
but I just want to underline
how much collaborative work

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:35.252
it's taken you all to make
this past year a reality.

00:17:35.252 --> 00:17:37.283
And I want to say I'm amazed by it.

00:17:39.690 --> 00:17:42.911
I feel like sometimes the
subtext of education is

00:17:42.911 --> 00:17:46.430
become well-rounded, be
skilled in everything.

00:17:46.430 --> 00:17:51.370
Don't have weaknesses, be a
Rhodes Scholar, be incredible,

00:17:51.370 --> 00:17:53.740
which I think in many
ways is a great message

00:17:53.740 --> 00:17:56.630
and that it leads to you
working to better yourself,

00:17:56.630 --> 00:17:58.520
which is really important.

00:17:58.520 --> 00:18:02.050
But at some point, you know,
everyone has weaknesses.

00:18:02.050 --> 00:18:03.283
It's called being human.

00:18:04.330 --> 00:18:06.750
After you graduate, you may struggle

00:18:06.750 --> 00:18:08.980
in the real world for a few years.

00:18:08.980 --> 00:18:10.630
I certainly did.

00:18:10.630 --> 00:18:13.205
I bumped up big-time into my weaknesses.

00:18:13.205 --> 00:18:16.660
I tend to be shy, an introvert.

00:18:16.660 --> 00:18:18.623
My daughter says I'm INFP.

00:18:19.470 --> 00:18:22.320
My wife says I'm a typical Pisces.

00:18:22.320 --> 00:18:23.230
And when I graduated,

00:18:23.230 --> 00:18:26.410
the buzzword for how to
get ahead was networking.

00:18:26.410 --> 00:18:29.210
Everyone said network, network, network,

00:18:29.210 --> 00:18:31.460
which to me sounded awful.

00:18:31.460 --> 00:18:33.330
The thought of going to cocktail parties

00:18:33.330 --> 00:18:36.400
and walking up to people
and giving them my card,

00:18:36.400 --> 00:18:39.350
I would just rather do
anything other than that.

00:18:39.350 --> 00:18:41.410
My other weakness was that I still

00:18:41.410 --> 00:18:43.990
wasn't fully baked as a songwriter.

00:18:43.990 --> 00:18:45.440
Since I was 13 years old,

00:18:45.440 --> 00:18:47.490
I had been writing songs
that were very good,

00:18:47.490 --> 00:18:50.890
but very good imitations
of Stephen Sondheim.

00:18:50.890 --> 00:18:51.723
Like this one.

00:18:52.660 --> 00:18:55.359
♪ Ladies and gentlemen, I've
got wonderful news for you ♪

00:18:55.359 --> 00:18:58.838
♪ We're about to start ♪

00:18:58.838 --> 00:19:01.590
I didn't yet have a voice of my own.

00:19:01.590 --> 00:19:04.610
So even if I did give someone
my card or my demo tape

00:19:04.610 --> 00:19:07.450
and they heard my songs,
they'd be like, "That's great.

00:19:07.450 --> 00:19:09.100
It sounds like Stephen Sondheim."

00:19:10.480 --> 00:19:13.840
It's okay to have weaknesses.

00:19:13.840 --> 00:19:15.690
It's okay to be shy.

00:19:15.690 --> 00:19:18.810
It's okay to not be fully
baked as a songwriter.

00:19:18.810 --> 00:19:20.340
It's okay to have a hard time

00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:23.059
writing a 25-minute long speech,

00:19:23.059 --> 00:19:27.600
because if you find community
and if you find collaborators,

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:29.750
you can partner with people who are strong

00:19:29.750 --> 00:19:31.243
in the areas you struggle in.

00:19:32.840 --> 00:19:35.160
I joined a class that taught
musical theater writing

00:19:35.160 --> 00:19:38.480
called the BMI Musical Theatre Workshop.

00:19:38.480 --> 00:19:41.370
And my classmates in that
class became my friends

00:19:41.370 --> 00:19:45.920
and collaborators, and helped
me find my voice as a writer.

00:19:45.920 --> 00:19:48.100
My collaborator Jeff Marx
was the kind of person

00:19:48.100 --> 00:19:51.780
who thrived on networking,
who loved promoting our team

00:19:51.780 --> 00:19:55.470
and never felt self-conscious
handing anyone a demo tape.

00:19:55.470 --> 00:19:57.390
Writing in the same room with Jeff

00:19:57.390 --> 00:20:00.290
brought out a different
side of my personality,

00:20:00.290 --> 00:20:02.580
one that was more like
who I was making jokes

00:20:02.580 --> 00:20:04.630
and laughing with the Spizzwinks at Yale.

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:08.783
So instead of my default, which
was to write like Sondheim,

00:20:08.783 --> 00:20:11.770
I ended up having a lot more fun.

00:20:11.770 --> 00:20:14.943
And what we wrote came
out fresher and more real.

00:20:15.820 --> 00:20:18.146
And together, we wrote
a show that was inspired

00:20:18.146 --> 00:20:20.227
by a lot of the feelings
we were both having

00:20:20.227 --> 00:20:22.503
struggling in the real world.

00:20:23.465 --> 00:20:25.393
And that show went to Broadway.

00:20:26.450 --> 00:20:28.220
In that show, "Avenue Q,"

00:20:28.220 --> 00:20:30.740
a puppet has just graduated college

00:20:30.740 --> 00:20:33.090
and is standing on the
stage in cap and gown

00:20:33.090 --> 00:20:35.020
holding his degree.

00:20:35.020 --> 00:20:37.560
He is meant to be just like you guys,

00:20:37.560 --> 00:20:39.790
except a little bit lamer.

00:20:39.790 --> 00:20:42.103
His name is Princeton.

00:20:42.954 --> 00:20:45.537
(gentle music)

00:20:49.404 --> 00:20:54.404
♪ What do you do with a B.A. in English ♪

00:20:55.631 --> 00:21:00.631
♪ What is my life going to be ♪

00:21:01.443 --> 00:21:06.443
♪ Four years of college
and plenty of knowledge ♪

00:21:07.422 --> 00:21:12.422
♪ Have earned me this useless degree ♪

00:21:13.245 --> 00:21:18.245
♪ I can't pay the bills yet
'cause I have no skills yet ♪

00:21:19.395 --> 00:21:24.395
♪ The world is a big scary place ♪

00:21:25.359 --> 00:21:30.359
♪ But somehow I can't shake
the feeling I might make ♪

00:21:32.511 --> 00:21:36.594
♪ A difference to the human race ♪

00:21:43.130 --> 00:21:45.440
I think in this moment of commencement

00:21:45.440 --> 00:21:48.450
and a new chapter of life
beginning for all of you,

00:21:48.450 --> 00:21:51.110
there's also the illusion of
a starter pistol going off

00:21:51.110 --> 00:21:53.520
and a mad dash to market yourself

00:21:53.520 --> 00:21:56.320
and sell what you have and what you are.

00:21:56.320 --> 00:21:59.080
And there's a lot of
truth to this metaphor.

00:21:59.080 --> 00:22:01.493
And a lot of what's great
about America lies in that race

00:22:01.493 --> 00:22:04.800
and the spirit of trying
to outshine the competition

00:22:04.800 --> 00:22:06.773
and sparkle the brightest.

00:22:06.773 --> 00:22:10.810
Many, if not all of you are
indeed special, sparkly diamonds

00:22:10.810 --> 00:22:13.150
discovered by the Yale
admissions department

00:22:13.150 --> 00:22:15.810
and cut and polished by
four years of education,

00:22:15.810 --> 00:22:19.080
now looking to be set in
gorgeous fine jewelry.

00:22:19.080 --> 00:22:21.410
Some of you may prefer
a more masculine image,

00:22:21.410 --> 00:22:23.860
and you can feel free to
invent that on your own.

00:22:24.803 --> 00:22:28.030
Any rate, what I'm trying to
say is that talent, craft,

00:22:28.030 --> 00:22:31.583
and ability need to be
channeled into purpose.

00:22:32.640 --> 00:22:34.980
Of course, you are all
talented in your particular way

00:22:34.980 --> 00:22:36.940
or you wouldn't be here.

00:22:36.940 --> 00:22:39.160
For me, I tried to write
the cleverest lyrics

00:22:39.160 --> 00:22:41.507
with puns and jokes and inner rhymes.

00:22:41.507 --> 00:22:43.340
I would search for new chords

00:22:43.340 --> 00:22:45.420
I'd never heard before in a song,

00:22:45.420 --> 00:22:49.800
musical flourishes and crazy
rhythms that would stand out.

00:22:49.800 --> 00:22:52.250
And now when I listen to
that old stuff, I cringe,

00:22:52.250 --> 00:22:53.590
because it's just all about trying to

00:22:53.590 --> 00:22:55.543
attract attention and show off.

00:22:56.440 --> 00:22:59.122
The subtext of it was "hire me."

00:22:59.122 --> 00:23:01.030
It was an immature way of writing

00:23:01.030 --> 00:23:03.743
and it had no purpose beyond itself.

00:23:04.740 --> 00:23:07.200
The true purpose of music
is not to serve yourself,

00:23:07.200 --> 00:23:09.690
but give something real and emotional

00:23:09.690 --> 00:23:11.103
to your fellow human being.

00:23:12.340 --> 00:23:15.460
And I learned this not from my
parents, not from a teacher,

00:23:15.460 --> 00:23:18.680
not from a friend, but
from a paper I wrote

00:23:18.680 --> 00:23:22.309
about Shakespeare's "Tempest"
in my senior year of Yale.

00:23:22.309 --> 00:23:24.530
This was my senior thesis in English,

00:23:24.530 --> 00:23:26.480
and I promise you I did not approach it

00:23:26.480 --> 00:23:29.057
with the idea of learning anything.

00:23:29.057 --> 00:23:31.450
I was just trying to
fulfill the requirement,

00:23:31.450 --> 00:23:33.623
get my degree, and get on with it.

00:23:35.570 --> 00:23:38.050
So in "The Tempest," there's
a character named Prospero,

00:23:38.050 --> 00:23:41.750
a powerful magician who
lives in exile on an island.

00:23:41.750 --> 00:23:43.490
And he has this servant named Ariel

00:23:43.490 --> 00:23:46.660
who is sort of a fairy spirit made of air.

00:23:46.660 --> 00:23:49.330
Prospero sends Ariel out
to do his magical bidding

00:23:49.330 --> 00:23:51.980
and influence the other
characters on the island,

00:23:51.980 --> 00:23:53.510
his purpose being complicated,

00:23:53.510 --> 00:23:56.710
but basically to redeem
himself by creating harmony

00:23:56.710 --> 00:23:58.881
in the lives of the other characters.

00:23:58.881 --> 00:24:00.640
And Ariel is always singing,

00:24:00.640 --> 00:24:02.653
and in many ways represents music.

00:24:03.660 --> 00:24:06.070
In my research, I found
that in the Renaissance,

00:24:06.070 --> 00:24:11.070
the line between academic
professor and magician was blurry,

00:24:11.070 --> 00:24:14.330
as was the line between music and magic.

00:24:14.330 --> 00:24:15.590
And I came across the work of

00:24:15.590 --> 00:24:18.300
a Renaissance philosopher named Ficino,

00:24:18.300 --> 00:24:20.640
who was famous for singing magical songs

00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:24.660
and whose idea was that music
was a form of airy spirit,

00:24:24.660 --> 00:24:28.222
kind of like Ariel, that could
fly between the singer and us

00:24:28.222 --> 00:24:30.600
and influence our own spirits,

00:24:30.600 --> 00:24:33.263
because both were made
of the same thing, air.

00:24:34.200 --> 00:24:37.200
The word "inspire" itself was
very central to all of this.

00:24:37.200 --> 00:24:39.573
Anyway, I don't think
it was a great paper.

00:24:40.470 --> 00:24:42.280
I didn't even pick it up to see my grade

00:24:42.280 --> 00:24:44.405
or read the comments because
I was kind of ashamed

00:24:44.405 --> 00:24:47.087
of how half-baked it was.

00:24:47.087 --> 00:24:49.450
But for the rest of my life,

00:24:49.450 --> 00:24:51.870
every time I've sat down to write a song,

00:24:51.870 --> 00:24:54.793
it's never been about trying
to get hired or get noticed.

00:24:55.740 --> 00:24:59.156
I think about the magic
power that music has to heal,

00:24:59.156 --> 00:25:03.150
to inspire, to influence, to ensnare,

00:25:03.150 --> 00:25:06.163
all those things that Prospero
could do, music can do.

00:25:07.190 --> 00:25:08.820
And ever since I started doing that,

00:25:08.820 --> 00:25:11.067
ever since I started trying
to reach out with music

00:25:11.067 --> 00:25:14.320
and help people with it, like magic,

00:25:14.320 --> 00:25:15.920
I started being more successful.

00:25:17.680 --> 00:25:19.040
I think this is true of anything

00:25:19.040 --> 00:25:21.170
any of you want to do in life.

00:25:21.170 --> 00:25:24.010
It needs to be of
service to the community.

00:25:24.010 --> 00:25:25.860
It needs to fulfill a function,

00:25:25.860 --> 00:25:28.193
to make people's lives better in some way.

00:25:29.124 --> 00:25:31.670
And if it does that, people will value it

00:25:31.670 --> 00:25:33.233
and people will value you.

00:25:34.730 --> 00:25:36.540
Kristen and I were
thinking about this idea

00:25:36.540 --> 00:25:39.930
when we wrote the song
"Remember Me" from "Coco."

00:25:39.930 --> 00:25:41.140
The first time we heard it,

00:25:41.140 --> 00:25:42.330
first time we hear it in the movie,

00:25:42.330 --> 00:25:44.010
the great Ernesto de la Cruz

00:25:44.010 --> 00:25:46.250
is singing a farewell to the audience,

00:25:46.250 --> 00:25:48.494
showing off for the
ladies and hamming it up.

00:25:48.494 --> 00:25:50.464
♪ Remember me ♪

00:25:50.464 --> 00:25:51.870
That kind of thing.

00:25:51.870 --> 00:25:53.550
But later we hear, spoiler alert,

00:25:53.550 --> 00:25:56.300
that the song was not written by Ernesto

00:25:56.300 --> 00:25:58.270
but by a poor musician named Hector

00:25:58.270 --> 00:26:00.243
that was forced to leave
his wife and little daughter

00:26:00.243 --> 00:26:02.077
when he went out on the road,

00:26:02.077 --> 00:26:05.055
and wrote her a lullaby to
make sure she always knew

00:26:05.055 --> 00:26:08.333
he always loved her even when he was away.

00:26:09.603 --> 00:26:13.513
♪ Remember me ♪

00:26:13.513 --> 00:26:15.900
♪ Though I have to say goodbye ♪

00:26:15.900 --> 00:26:19.275
♪ Remember me ♪

00:26:19.275 --> 00:26:21.898
♪ Don't let it make you cry ♪

00:26:21.898 --> 00:26:26.898
♪ For even if I'm far away
I hold you in my heart ♪

00:26:27.441 --> 00:26:32.441
♪ I sing a secret song to
you each night we are apart ♪

00:26:32.643 --> 00:26:35.984
♪ Remember me ♪

00:26:35.984 --> 00:26:38.893
♪ Though I have to travel far ♪

00:26:38.893 --> 00:26:41.623
♪ Remember me ♪

00:26:41.623 --> 00:26:45.693
♪ Each time you hear a sad guitar ♪

00:26:45.693 --> 00:26:50.693
♪ Know that I'm with you
the only way that I can be ♪

00:26:54.023 --> 00:26:59.023
♪ Until you're in my arms again ♪

00:26:59.721 --> 00:27:02.221
♪ Remember me ♪

00:27:09.840 --> 00:27:11.942
So when you sit down to work
on something with someone,

00:27:11.942 --> 00:27:14.350
what's the worst thing that can happen&gt;

00:27:14.350 --> 00:27:16.962
Is it that the work goes badly,
that you waste a few hours

00:27:16.962 --> 00:27:20.981
or even a whole day making
something you aren't proud of?

00:27:20.981 --> 00:27:23.390
Or is it that you might hurt the feelings

00:27:23.390 --> 00:27:25.340
of the person you're working with?

00:27:25.340 --> 00:27:27.480
So the rule I had always operated by,

00:27:27.480 --> 00:27:30.380
which seemed logical to
my ambitious, driven,

00:27:30.380 --> 00:27:33.800
stressed-out Yale brain was
that the most important thing

00:27:33.800 --> 00:27:37.200
when you sit down to
write a song is the song.

00:27:37.200 --> 00:27:40.550
As long as the song turns
out great, anything goes,

00:27:40.550 --> 00:27:42.082
which meant for me and my collaborators,

00:27:42.082 --> 00:27:45.340
we had to have any power
struggle, painful criticism,

00:27:45.340 --> 00:27:49.410
or knock-down, drag-out fight
that could help the song.

00:27:49.410 --> 00:27:51.090
Things took as long as they took

00:27:51.090 --> 00:27:53.520
and our feelings weren't important.

00:27:53.520 --> 00:27:54.800
And operating by this rule,

00:27:54.800 --> 00:27:58.010
I wrote lots of good songs
with lots of great people,

00:27:58.010 --> 00:27:59.980
but I noticed that those collaborations

00:27:59.980 --> 00:28:02.830
tended to run into trouble long term

00:28:02.830 --> 00:28:05.080
when bad feelings would
accumulate over time.

00:28:07.630 --> 00:28:09.090
So one day Kristen and I were

00:28:09.090 --> 00:28:11.470
writing a song on a particular project

00:28:11.470 --> 00:28:14.150
and we were having our
usual fight for control.

00:28:14.150 --> 00:28:16.410
And I told myself "Today,

00:28:16.410 --> 00:28:18.890
Kristen matters more than this song.

00:28:18.890 --> 00:28:20.610
I won't stifle myself creatively,

00:28:20.610 --> 00:28:22.680
but I won't fight for anything.

00:28:22.680 --> 00:28:25.760
And we'll try every
idea that everyone has.

00:28:25.760 --> 00:28:28.135
Who cares if you write a bad song?"

00:28:28.135 --> 00:28:32.090
So Kristen said, "I want to use
the four chords of awesome."

00:28:32.090 --> 00:28:33.800
For those of you who don't know,

00:28:33.800 --> 00:28:35.320
those are the pop hit chords.

00:28:35.320 --> 00:28:40.180
The one, five, minor six, four.

00:28:40.180 --> 00:28:41.800
And I had never allowed us to use them

00:28:41.800 --> 00:28:45.700
because I thought they were
like musical MSG, just too easy.

00:28:45.700 --> 00:28:47.570
But today we are gonna try every idea.

00:28:47.570 --> 00:28:49.990
I knew she was going for something.

00:28:49.990 --> 00:28:53.400
And this is what we wrote when
I decided to treat Kristen

00:28:53.400 --> 00:28:55.800
as more important than
the song we were writing.

00:28:56.704 --> 00:28:59.967
♪ Let it go, let it go ♪

00:28:59.967 --> 00:29:03.325
♪ I am one with the wind and sky ♪

00:29:03.325 --> 00:29:06.907
♪ Let it go, let it go ♪

00:29:06.907 --> 00:29:10.698
♪ You'll never see me cry ♪

00:29:10.698 --> 00:29:15.698
♪ Here I stand and here I'll stay ♪

00:29:16.418 --> 00:29:19.986
♪ Let the storm rage on ♪

00:29:19.986 --> 00:29:24.986
♪ My power flurries through
the air into the ground ♪

00:29:26.569 --> 00:29:31.569
♪ My soul is spiraling in
frozen fractals all around ♪

00:29:32.827 --> 00:29:37.827
♪ And one thought crystallizes
like an icy blast ♪

00:29:39.178 --> 00:29:44.178
♪ I'm never going back,
the past is in the past ♪

00:29:46.576 --> 00:29:50.016
♪ Let it go, let it go ♪

00:29:50.016 --> 00:29:53.359
♪ And I'll rise like the break of dawn ♪

00:29:53.359 --> 00:29:56.728
♪ Let it go, let it go ♪

00:29:56.728 --> 00:30:00.600
♪ That perfect girl is gone ♪

00:30:00.600 --> 00:30:05.600
♪ Here I stand in the light of day ♪

00:30:08.547 --> 00:30:13.547
♪ Let the storm rage on ♪

00:30:13.929 --> 00:30:18.262
♪ The cold never bothered me anyway ♪

00:30:20.110 --> 00:30:21.390
That song was more fun to write

00:30:21.390 --> 00:30:23.280
than anything we'd ever written.

00:30:23.280 --> 00:30:25.980
It was a joyful, happy process,

00:30:25.980 --> 00:30:28.170
and it allowed Kristen's
perspective as an artist

00:30:28.170 --> 00:30:30.733
and a feminist to change
a world a teeny bit.

00:30:31.627 --> 00:30:34.560
The people you work with
are always more important

00:30:34.560 --> 00:30:36.280
than the work you're doing.

00:30:36.280 --> 00:30:39.103
There is only benefit when
you value them that way.

00:30:41.530 --> 00:30:45.669
So I've talked a lot
today about collaboration.

00:30:45.669 --> 00:30:47.580
I hope it's been helpful

00:30:47.580 --> 00:30:50.740
and I hope you go out there
and knock our socks off.

00:30:50.740 --> 00:30:52.510
I know you will.

00:30:52.510 --> 00:30:54.210
Like everything else I've ever done,

00:30:54.210 --> 00:30:56.220
I had help with this, and I want to end

00:30:56.220 --> 00:30:59.580
by thanking everyone who helped
me get this speech together.

00:30:59.580 --> 00:31:02.600
Yale and President Salovey
for asking me to do it.

00:31:02.600 --> 00:31:05.270
Alison Coleman and the students
on the Class Day Committee

00:31:05.270 --> 00:31:09.970
who coached me on it, my
friends, my family, my kids,

00:31:09.970 --> 00:31:12.820
and especially my closest collaborator,

00:31:12.820 --> 00:31:15.990
my partner and wife,
Kristen Anderson-Lopez.

00:31:15.990 --> 00:31:18.470
No Kristen, no speech.

00:31:18.470 --> 00:31:21.520
And finally, that squirrel
from my dorm room?

00:31:21.520 --> 00:31:23.590
Well, it's taken me 24 years,

00:31:23.590 --> 00:31:26.601
but to thank him, I
finally memorialized him

00:31:26.601 --> 00:31:29.260
with his own Disney-style song

00:31:29.260 --> 00:31:31.610
where he sings his message to me,

00:31:31.610 --> 00:31:33.420
and I'd like to sing it now for you

00:31:33.420 --> 00:31:36.420
to close this out as sort
of a squirrely benediction

00:31:36.420 --> 00:31:38.393
on what has been an amazing day.

00:31:39.787 --> 00:31:44.359
♪ Don't give up when you're stuck ♪

00:31:44.359 --> 00:31:48.358
♪ Take my help, what the ___ ♪

00:31:48.358 --> 00:31:52.420
♪ I may be just a
squirrel, but dammit girl ♪

00:31:52.420 --> 00:31:55.011
♪ I'll help you through ♪

00:31:55.011 --> 00:31:57.318
♪ Help me help you ♪

00:31:57.318 --> 00:32:01.740
♪ Don't be above collaboration ♪

00:32:01.740 --> 00:32:05.820
♪ After your graduation ♪

00:32:05.820 --> 00:32:08.371
♪ Be someone else's squirrel ♪

00:32:08.371 --> 00:32:12.713
♪ Give that tail a swirl
and help them too ♪

00:32:12.713 --> 00:32:15.108
♪ Help them help you ♪

00:32:15.108 --> 00:32:19.345
♪ You've been through more than anyone ♪

00:32:19.345 --> 00:32:24.345
♪ Yale College Class of '21 ♪

00:32:25.745 --> 00:32:30.407
♪ Bright college years
with pleasure rife ♪

00:32:30.407 --> 00:32:34.900
♪ The shortest, gladdest years of life ♪

00:32:34.900 --> 00:32:38.978
♪ Now go into the world,
but do it squirreled ♪

00:32:38.978 --> 00:32:43.298
♪ You'll sail right through if you ♪

00:32:43.298 --> 00:32:46.381
♪ Help them help you ♪

00:32:49.858 --> 00:32:52.441
(bright music)

00:33:00.770 --> 00:33:02.180
Boola boola!

00:33:02.180 --> 00:33:04.170
- Hello, Class of 2021.

00:33:04.170 --> 00:33:06.157
I'm Bryan Owens, your class secretary.

00:33:06.157 --> 00:33:08.930
- And I'm Curtis Colvett,
your class treasurer.

00:33:08.930 --> 00:33:12.020
- And we are both very excited
to share this day with you.

00:33:12.020 --> 00:33:14.560
- Class of 2021, what
a privilege it's been

00:33:14.560 --> 00:33:18.930
to be the historic largest class
ever to enter Yale College.

00:33:18.930 --> 00:33:21.783
We were essentially the
vanguard of a new era at Yale,

00:33:21.783 --> 00:33:24.630
a new era not because Yale
is fundamentally changed,

00:33:24.630 --> 00:33:26.178
but because it has grown.

00:33:26.178 --> 00:33:28.690
Become more inclusive, more accessible,

00:33:28.690 --> 00:33:31.210
to offer more opportunity for all.

00:33:31.210 --> 00:33:34.640
Case in point, even in
2017, when we were freshmen,

00:33:34.640 --> 00:33:39.330
Durfee Market expanded its
meal swipe from $8 to $9

00:33:39.330 --> 00:33:42.140
and many other wonderful
changes and developments

00:33:42.140 --> 00:33:44.100
have been happening all around us.

00:33:44.100 --> 00:33:46.390
We were the first class
to live all four years

00:33:46.390 --> 00:33:49.180
in Ben Franklin and Pauli Murray Colleges.

00:33:49.180 --> 00:33:51.890
Simultaneously, facilities
all around campus

00:33:51.890 --> 00:33:54.150
have been expanding and
shifting their roles.

00:33:54.150 --> 00:33:55.990
Schwarzman Center, which has been closed

00:33:55.990 --> 00:33:57.760
essentially since we've been here

00:33:57.760 --> 00:33:59.040
will nonetheless open very soon

00:33:59.040 --> 00:34:01.600
to students for generations to come.

00:34:01.600 --> 00:34:04.240
Science Hill has all
kinds of new facilities

00:34:04.240 --> 00:34:05.830
for state-of-the-art research.

00:34:05.830 --> 00:34:08.710
The Tobin Economic Center will soon open.

00:34:08.710 --> 00:34:10.430
The future of Yale looks really bright

00:34:10.430 --> 00:34:12.730
and it's a privilege to have
been one of the first classes

00:34:12.730 --> 00:34:15.199
to experience Yale in this new phase.

00:34:15.199 --> 00:34:17.550
- When we think back to Yale,

00:34:17.550 --> 00:34:20.230
we will inevitably remember
the trials and tribulations

00:34:20.230 --> 00:34:21.730
of the past four years,

00:34:21.730 --> 00:34:24.640
and especially of the
past year and a half.

00:34:24.640 --> 00:34:27.770
Our class braved this
once-in-a-century pandemic

00:34:27.770 --> 00:34:29.770
and we've grown resilient because of it.

00:34:32.040 --> 00:34:35.093
We created ways to ensure
that social distancing

00:34:35.093 --> 00:34:37.853
does not mean becoming antisocial.

00:34:38.927 --> 00:34:40.440
"Let's catch up after the pandemic"

00:34:40.440 --> 00:34:42.640
became the new "Let's
grab a meal sometime."

00:34:43.950 --> 00:34:46.660
We survived months of Zoom lectures,

00:34:46.660 --> 00:34:50.693
lying in bed half awake, camera
off and microphone muted.

00:34:52.020 --> 00:34:54.320
- And this weekend we're
gonna be saying goodbye

00:34:54.320 --> 00:34:57.540
to our time living in these
14 residential colleges.

00:34:57.540 --> 00:35:00.450
The memories and the friendships
are gonna last a lifetime,

00:35:00.450 --> 00:35:02.600
but this constant
closeness of being together

00:35:02.600 --> 00:35:06.033
in one nice beautiful town is going away.

00:35:06.880 --> 00:35:09.250
We'll remember the professors
who have shaped our lives,

00:35:09.250 --> 00:35:12.120
the faculty and staff who
have taken care of us,

00:35:12.120 --> 00:35:15.570
and all those conversations
that we had at random times

00:35:15.570 --> 00:35:19.510
in the day and night, being
in the basement of Bass

00:35:19.510 --> 00:35:22.570
or being in the slightly
less dark upstairs of Bass,

00:35:22.570 --> 00:35:25.460
possibly even walking down
what Mark Twain once called

00:35:25.460 --> 00:35:28.210
the most beautiful street in
America, Hillhouse Avenue.

00:35:31.500 --> 00:35:34.340
- Many of us will feel a wistful emptiness

00:35:34.340 --> 00:35:37.290
as we lose our core identity
of being a Yale student,

00:35:37.290 --> 00:35:40.010
but our connections with
Yale will only grow stronger

00:35:40.010 --> 00:35:43.083
as we build our new
identities as Yale alumni.

00:35:44.050 --> 00:35:47.094
We will join a group of
people dedicated to service,

00:35:47.094 --> 00:35:49.814
leadership, and education.

00:35:49.814 --> 00:35:52.233
And if there's anything
that COVID has taught us,

00:35:52.233 --> 00:35:54.383
it's that we are all in this together.

00:35:55.360 --> 00:35:57.860
So please, stay in touch with the people

00:35:57.860 --> 00:35:59.260
who have touched your lives,

00:36:01.600 --> 00:36:04.810
and at the same time make new friends

00:36:04.810 --> 00:36:07.733
at your regional Yale Clubs
and alumni interest groups.

00:36:08.580 --> 00:36:10.450
Even consider becoming a mentor

00:36:10.450 --> 00:36:12.460
to the next classes of Yalies.

00:36:12.460 --> 00:36:14.740
And of course, we welcome you to join us

00:36:14.740 --> 00:36:17.950
at our Class of 2021 alumni events.

00:36:17.950 --> 00:36:20.012
We hope to see you there.

00:36:20.012 --> 00:36:21.870
We'd like to take one last second to thank

00:36:21.870 --> 00:36:25.060
the family and friends who
devoted their time and energy

00:36:25.060 --> 00:36:27.300
to making us into the
adults that we are today.

00:36:27.300 --> 00:36:31.780
They've shaped the Class of
2021 in so many wonderful ways.

00:36:31.780 --> 00:36:33.980
We'd also like to think
Yale administration

00:36:33.980 --> 00:36:36.320
for sticking with us this difficult year,

00:36:36.320 --> 00:36:38.270
helping us to make the most out of it,

00:36:38.270 --> 00:36:40.150
for ensuring that we had a safe, fun,

00:36:40.150 --> 00:36:42.090
and memorable senior year.

00:36:42.090 --> 00:36:45.250
But above all, it's been
our pleasure to serve

00:36:45.250 --> 00:36:47.773
as your senior class officers.

00:36:48.810 --> 00:36:49.740
- Once again.

00:36:49.740 --> 00:36:51.930
- [Both] Congratulations and good luck

00:36:51.930 --> 00:36:53.340
to the Class of 2021.

00:36:57.540 --> 00:36:58.373
- Hey, everyone.

00:36:58.373 --> 00:36:59.400
Obviously Harvard-Yale

00:36:59.400 --> 00:37:01.260
is one of the best weekends of the year,

00:37:01.260 --> 00:37:03.680
and some of my fondest memories
from first year, actually,

00:37:03.680 --> 00:37:06.220
with Angelica and
Lauren, was waiting in line

00:37:06.220 --> 00:37:08.620
for the bus to the
stadium for over an hour.

00:37:08.620 --> 00:37:11.060
And I may or may not
have ordered and canceled

00:37:11.060 --> 00:37:12.303
three or four Ubers.

00:37:13.380 --> 00:37:15.040
- It's just one of those Yale traditions

00:37:15.040 --> 00:37:17.080
that really brings everyone together.

00:37:17.080 --> 00:37:19.330
You look up when you're
sitting in the student section

00:37:19.330 --> 00:37:21.680
and realize that you're part
of this amazing community

00:37:21.680 --> 00:37:23.640
and that you have so
much to be grateful for.

00:37:23.640 --> 00:37:25.210
- And of course we all
missed it this year,

00:37:25.210 --> 00:37:26.870
but we will for sure be back next,

00:37:26.870 --> 00:37:30.725
remembering that the weekend
is a marathon and not a sprint.

00:37:30.725 --> 00:37:33.308
(upbeat music)

00:38:24.030 --> 00:38:26.160
- I have the pleasure of
introducing this year's speakers

00:38:26.160 --> 00:38:27.730
for the comedic reflection.

00:38:27.730 --> 00:38:30.840
Teava Torres de Sa from
Hopper College is graduating

00:38:30.840 --> 00:38:33.520
with a bachelor of science in mathematics.

00:38:33.520 --> 00:38:35.630
Matt Nadel, also from Hopper College,

00:38:35.630 --> 00:38:38.440
is graduating with a bachelor
of arts in American studies,

00:38:38.440 --> 00:38:41.260
which he insists is just
as employable as math.

00:38:41.260 --> 00:38:42.470
The two of them wrote the speech

00:38:42.470 --> 00:38:44.450
in their dark basement apartment,

00:38:44.450 --> 00:38:47.325
fueled by an unending
passion for the Class of 2021

00:38:47.325 --> 00:38:49.540
and a group of friends
who contributed jokes

00:38:49.540 --> 00:38:52.090
and offered edits on every draft.

00:38:52.090 --> 00:38:53.700
When asked about their writing process,

00:38:53.700 --> 00:38:56.210
Matt and Teava said, "We wrote many jokes,

00:38:56.210 --> 00:38:57.970
and to the relief of
the Class Day committee,

00:38:57.970 --> 00:39:00.180
only some of them are in this speech."

00:39:00.180 --> 00:39:02.640
But I have to say, having
seen the two of them

00:39:02.640 --> 00:39:05.290
work together on the speech,
the only thing that's stronger

00:39:05.290 --> 00:39:06.808
than the jokes you're about to hear

00:39:06.808 --> 00:39:09.770
is their obvious love for
each other as suitemates.

00:39:09.770 --> 00:39:12.270
So with that, I'll turn
it over to Matt and Teava

00:39:12.270 --> 00:39:14.750
to deliver this year's comedic reflection.

00:39:14.750 --> 00:39:17.400
- President Salovey, Dean Chun,

00:39:17.400 --> 00:39:19.870
esteemed administrators and faculty,

00:39:19.870 --> 00:39:24.270
Yale friends and family, and
members of the Class of 2021,

00:39:24.270 --> 00:39:26.910
today is a day for pride:

00:39:26.910 --> 00:39:30.230
pride in our alma mater,
pride in our classmates,

00:39:30.230 --> 00:39:31.870
and pride in ourselves.

00:39:31.870 --> 00:39:34.490
- I, for one, am a proud Latino Yalie,

00:39:34.490 --> 00:39:36.760
child of immigrants, who made
his way to the Ivy League

00:39:36.760 --> 00:39:37.760
against all odds.

00:39:37.760 --> 00:39:38.913
- And I, um.

00:39:42.600 --> 00:39:44.370
Yeah, I'm actually just gonna go.

00:39:44.370 --> 00:39:45.520
- Matt, come back!

00:39:45.520 --> 00:39:47.760
You have so much to be proud of!

00:39:47.760 --> 00:39:50.210
You're a great student. You're funny.

00:39:50.210 --> 00:39:52.253
You're a pillar of the LGBT community.

00:39:54.290 --> 00:39:55.980
- You think I'm a pillar?

00:39:55.980 --> 00:39:57.560
- It's good to have you back.

00:39:57.560 --> 00:39:59.370
Matt and I have known each
other for quite some time.

00:39:59.370 --> 00:40:02.600
- In fact, we met on August 25th, 2017,

00:40:02.600 --> 00:40:04.830
the first day of our first year at Yale.

00:40:04.830 --> 00:40:06.330
- We were randomly assigned roommates

00:40:06.330 --> 00:40:08.370
coming to Yale from opposite
sides of the country.

00:40:08.370 --> 00:40:09.740
- I'm sure you remember the excitement

00:40:09.740 --> 00:40:11.400
of meeting your first-year roommate.

00:40:11.400 --> 00:40:12.680
Your dad was in the common room

00:40:12.680 --> 00:40:15.140
putting together a Sandbacken from Ikea.

00:40:15.140 --> 00:40:18.060
- Your mom was disinfecting
every bathroom in Bingham Hall.

00:40:18.060 --> 00:40:20.160
- And you were beginning
a lifelong friendship

00:40:20.160 --> 00:40:22.400
that would definitely
never become hostile.

00:40:22.400 --> 00:40:23.860
- I certainly remember it.

00:40:23.860 --> 00:40:26.050
For me and Matt, it was
love at first sight.

00:40:26.050 --> 00:40:30.640
I mean, Tinder? Ship?
Datamatch? Forget 'em.

00:40:30.640 --> 00:40:32.130
If you really want to find love,

00:40:32.130 --> 00:40:33.690
put your faith in Jeremiah Quinlan

00:40:33.690 --> 00:40:35.390
and the Yale housing algorithm.

00:40:35.390 --> 00:40:38.620
- To clarify, we're not actually
lovers. We're best friends.

00:40:38.620 --> 00:40:40.190
- In fact, a few months after meeting Matt

00:40:40.190 --> 00:40:42.190
I started dating his best friend

00:40:42.190 --> 00:40:44.020
and we've been together ever since.

00:40:44.020 --> 00:40:46.380
Boy, has that been convenient
for the three of us.

00:40:46.380 --> 00:40:49.530
- Yup, the three of us. All three of us.

00:40:49.530 --> 00:40:52.523
Convenient for all three of us equally.

00:40:54.270 --> 00:40:56.271
Unlike Teava, I didn't
have the good fortune

00:40:56.271 --> 00:40:58.830
of finding true love in
my first year at Yale.

00:40:58.830 --> 00:40:59.663
I trust people have seen

00:40:59.663 --> 00:41:01.960
"To All The Boys I've Loved Before."

00:41:01.960 --> 00:41:03.160
My love life was more like

00:41:03.160 --> 00:41:05.300
"To All The Boys I've Met At Woads

00:41:05.300 --> 00:41:08.820
Who Puked On My Rug And Cried
About Their Ex In my Bed."

00:41:08.820 --> 00:41:11.690
I mean, clearly one in four is not enough.

00:41:11.690 --> 00:41:13.206
- Oh, Matt, come on. It's their loss.

00:41:13.206 --> 00:41:14.440
You're a catch.

00:41:14.440 --> 00:41:15.500
- Thank you.

00:41:15.500 --> 00:41:18.004
I have to admit, I was
jealous our first semester

00:41:18.004 --> 00:41:20.237
when I opened "Rumpus" and saw Teava

00:41:20.237 --> 00:41:23.160
on the 50 Most Beautiful
People at Yale list.

00:41:23.160 --> 00:41:25.640
But now I understand my worth.

00:41:25.640 --> 00:41:30.640
I may not be 50 Most hot, but
I'm Haas Family Library hot,

00:41:30.640 --> 00:41:34.090
which is Sterling frumpy
and Bass Cafe basic.

00:41:34.090 --> 00:41:35.480
But CTL sexy.

00:41:35.480 --> 00:41:38.210
- Ah, the CTL, where I
pulled an all-nighter

00:41:38.210 --> 00:41:40.470
and had a meltdown in
front of my FOOT leader.

00:41:40.470 --> 00:41:43.600
- FOOT! I almost forgot!
Yale's favorite cult!

00:41:43.600 --> 00:41:47.180
Providing fertilizer to the
Appalachian Trail since 1701.

00:41:47.180 --> 00:41:50.080
You know, I almost did FOOT,
but actually broke my foot.

00:41:50.080 --> 00:41:51.240
- I'm sad you never got
to meet your FOOTies.

00:41:51.240 --> 00:41:54.250
- Right? But you know
who I did get to meet?

00:41:54.250 --> 00:41:55.550
My FroCo group.

00:41:55.550 --> 00:42:00.430
And my TF, and my ULA, and my
CCE, and my CAG, and my PL.

00:42:00.430 --> 00:42:01.263
- Peer liaison.

00:42:01.263 --> 00:42:02.500
- And my PL.
- Personal librarian.

00:42:02.500 --> 00:42:04.600
- And of course, my SSRI.

00:42:04.600 --> 00:42:06.090
- Selective serotonin rehabilitation?

00:42:06.090 --> 00:42:09.570
- The Summer Science
Research Institute. Yes, sir.

00:42:09.570 --> 00:42:10.586
And speaking of science,

00:42:10.586 --> 00:42:13.450
can I hear from all my pre-meds out there?

00:42:13.450 --> 00:42:16.340
- Look at you, with your lab
coats and employable skills.

00:42:16.340 --> 00:42:18.640
- I know you're at home,
but if you're pre-med,

00:42:18.640 --> 00:42:21.680
I want you to raise your hand proud.

00:42:21.680 --> 00:42:22.570
Great.

00:42:22.570 --> 00:42:24.690
Now please keep your hand
up if you are available

00:42:24.690 --> 00:42:26.720
for free or low cost tutoring.

00:42:26.720 --> 00:42:29.050
I am still trying to
finish my science credit.

00:42:29.050 --> 00:42:29.940
- Now, now, now.

00:42:29.940 --> 00:42:32.180
Matt's modest, so here's
something he won't tell you.

00:42:32.180 --> 00:42:33.980
He's an amazing student.

00:42:33.980 --> 00:42:37.090
Since meeting Matt, I now
have an organized calendar,

00:42:37.090 --> 00:42:41.630
better study habits, and
crippling imposter syndrome.

00:42:41.630 --> 00:42:43.270
You know what I'm talking about.

00:42:43.270 --> 00:42:45.610
Now, if Yale really
wants to make us normies

00:42:45.610 --> 00:42:47.150
feel comfortable on this campus,

00:42:47.150 --> 00:42:49.040
all they need to do is
put people like Matt

00:42:49.040 --> 00:42:51.970
in stand-alone singles and not
let them speak in sections.

00:42:51.970 --> 00:42:53.484
- And just to piggyback off that,

00:42:53.484 --> 00:42:55.359
I had some tough nights at Yale.

00:42:55.359 --> 00:42:57.230
I'm sure you did too.

00:42:57.230 --> 00:42:58.770
But in those moments of despair,

00:42:58.770 --> 00:43:02.530
we must remember we have a resource.

00:43:02.530 --> 00:43:03.740
The Good Life Center.

00:43:03.740 --> 00:43:05.760
- Feeling down? Play in a sandbox.

00:43:05.760 --> 00:43:07.940
- Accidentally called your professor Mom?

00:43:07.940 --> 00:43:09.000
Touch a succulent.

00:43:09.000 --> 00:43:11.390
- Submitted fanfiction
instead of an essay?

00:43:11.390 --> 00:43:13.110
Prof. Santos has chai on tap.

00:43:13.110 --> 00:43:14.865
- And you know who else
at Yale has got your back?

00:43:14.865 --> 00:43:17.593
Who will be there for you day and night?

00:43:19.070 --> 00:43:20.130
Your personal librarian.

00:43:20.130 --> 00:43:21.090
- Your personal librarian.

00:43:21.090 --> 00:43:22.960
- In fact, I, oh, Teava, should I?

00:43:22.960 --> 00:43:24.680
- Matt, she's gonna love it.
- Okay.

00:43:24.680 --> 00:43:27.360
I actually wrote a poem in
honor of my personal librarian.

00:43:27.360 --> 00:43:31.763
So, um, Lori Bronars,
this haiku is for you.

00:43:32.700 --> 00:43:33.533
(clearing throat)

00:43:33.533 --> 00:43:35.480
Orbis was no help.

00:43:35.480 --> 00:43:37.810
Needed newspaper records.

00:43:37.810 --> 00:43:39.210
Emailed you midnight

00:43:39.210 --> 00:43:41.500
please help, bad grade,
fail class, no job,

00:43:41.500 --> 00:43:43.560
law school, Dad's pride!
- All right!

00:43:43.560 --> 00:43:44.440
- You didn't like it?

00:43:44.440 --> 00:43:45.610
- I would critique your poetry

00:43:45.610 --> 00:43:47.997
but I haven't read a book
since I was 10 years old.

00:43:47.997 --> 00:43:49.570
- Teava!
- What?

00:43:49.570 --> 00:43:51.360
I expected to read books at Yale.

00:43:51.360 --> 00:43:52.900
I expected I'd spend hours reading

00:43:52.900 --> 00:43:55.100
the most prolific authors in history.

00:43:55.100 --> 00:43:58.057
William Shakespeare, Virginia
Woolf, Toni Morrison.

00:43:58.057 --> 00:44:01.490
But instead I spent hours
reading COVID emails

00:44:01.490 --> 00:44:04.480
from Melanie Boyd, Marvin
Chun and Stephanie Spangler.

00:44:04.480 --> 00:44:06.890
- And that's not Yale's
only false promise.

00:44:06.890 --> 00:44:09.290
Yale tells you that you'll
meet the next John Kerry

00:44:09.290 --> 00:44:11.920
or Meryl Streep, but
you're much more likely

00:44:11.920 --> 00:44:13.960
to meet their art major grandchild

00:44:13.960 --> 00:44:16.220
at a pregame you weren't invited to.

00:44:16.220 --> 00:44:18.385
- Yeah, I expected to
meet the rich and famous,

00:44:18.385 --> 00:44:21.110
but all I've gotten is a $6 Venmo request

00:44:21.110 --> 00:44:23.410
from a billionaire's child for Franzia.

00:44:23.410 --> 00:44:25.180
- Of course the biggest broken promise

00:44:25.180 --> 00:44:27.010
came on our first day at Yale.

00:44:27.010 --> 00:44:28.230
In a speech in Woolsey Hall,

00:44:28.230 --> 00:44:30.920
Dean Chun told us not to worry
about getting straight As.

00:44:30.920 --> 00:44:33.156
You can get a couple Bs and Cs.

00:44:33.156 --> 00:44:35.270
But what he didn't anticipate

00:44:35.270 --> 00:44:37.358
is that we'd all be getting Ps.

00:44:37.358 --> 00:44:39.550
- Thank you, No-Fail Yale.

00:44:39.550 --> 00:44:41.870
The pandemic has indeed
presented many obstacles,

00:44:41.870 --> 00:44:43.670
but we've adapted our senior year to Zoom.

00:44:43.670 --> 00:44:46.368
- We've gone to Zinprov
shows and Zoga classes.

00:44:46.368 --> 00:44:49.140
- Zaked parties and zisciplinary hearings.

00:44:49.140 --> 00:44:51.010
- But most of all, we've gone to

00:44:51.010 --> 00:44:53.250
Zale Zental Health and Zounseling.

00:44:53.250 --> 00:44:55.100
Group zerapy, of course.

00:44:55.100 --> 00:44:57.820
- We missed out on a lot
of senior traditions.

00:44:57.820 --> 00:45:01.400
Society parties, Masquerade,
Last Chance Dance.

00:45:01.400 --> 00:45:02.740
But if there's one senior tradition

00:45:02.740 --> 00:45:06.200
that's endured these tough
times, it's Feb Club.

00:45:06.200 --> 00:45:07.700
- You know what we're talking about.

00:45:07.700 --> 00:45:08.750
- And in our apartment,

00:45:08.750 --> 00:45:10.935
we found ourselves celebrating March Club.

00:45:10.935 --> 00:45:12.220
- April Club.

00:45:12.220 --> 00:45:15.710
- And we are in the midst of a
thrilling May Club right now.

00:45:15.710 --> 00:45:17.383
- Right now.

00:45:18.310 --> 00:45:19.640
But here's the thing.

00:45:19.640 --> 00:45:22.510
Even when we're zeniors no more,

00:45:22.510 --> 00:45:24.743
there is a club to which
we will all belong.

00:45:26.200 --> 00:45:28.730
- The Yale Club, where for $1,000 a night

00:45:28.730 --> 00:45:30.190
you can get a New York City hotel room

00:45:30.190 --> 00:45:32.040
that reminds you of a Welch single.

00:45:32.040 --> 00:45:36.770
- So win your Tonys, cure
your patients, save the world.

00:45:36.770 --> 00:45:39.090
The Class of 2021 will scatter the globe

00:45:39.090 --> 00:45:40.670
after we leave campus,

00:45:40.670 --> 00:45:43.010
but we will always be
united in our memories.

00:45:43.010 --> 00:45:45.500
- We will always be the
class that paid full tuition

00:45:45.500 --> 00:45:49.430
to watch a Sterling Professor
speak on mute for 15 minutes.

00:45:49.430 --> 00:45:51.770
- We'll always be the
class that lost Commons,

00:45:51.770 --> 00:45:55.400
Wall Street Pizza, and
of course, Myrtle Beach,

00:45:55.400 --> 00:45:59.467
which no one would go to against
COVID regulations, right?

00:46:02.580 --> 00:46:03.890
- And we'll always be the class

00:46:03.890 --> 00:46:05.900
that made it out in
one piece with the same

00:46:05.900 --> 00:46:08.490
top-of-the-line education
our predecessors enjoyed.

00:46:08.490 --> 00:46:11.490
- And some-top-of-the-line mRNA
they couldn't have imagined.

00:46:11.490 --> 00:46:12.323
- Thank you.

00:46:16.497 --> 00:46:18.690
- When I think about the
student organizations here,

00:46:18.690 --> 00:46:20.980
I think about family and camaraderie.

00:46:20.980 --> 00:46:23.150
I also think about the ways
in which we all come together

00:46:23.150 --> 00:46:24.640
from such diverse backgrounds

00:46:24.640 --> 00:46:27.310
to unite with the same
passions and interests.

00:46:27.310 --> 00:46:29.690
For me, I met some of my
dearest and closest friends

00:46:29.690 --> 00:46:31.710
throughout my time in these organizations

00:46:31.710 --> 00:46:33.890
and will forever be grateful for that.

00:46:33.890 --> 00:46:36.480
For me, two organizations
that stand out in particular

00:46:36.480 --> 00:46:38.498
are Danceworks and Yale Steppin' Out,

00:46:38.498 --> 00:46:40.490
because they're two
organizations that are part of

00:46:40.490 --> 00:46:42.480
the greater dance community here at Yale.

00:46:42.480 --> 00:46:44.650
And I'll really remember
every single rehearsal

00:46:44.650 --> 00:46:46.710
and performance I've done in these groups

00:46:46.710 --> 00:46:48.480
because these have really
helped me cultivate

00:46:48.480 --> 00:46:51.180
deeper and meaningful
connections with people.

00:46:51.180 --> 00:46:52.780
And I also think that there's
something so beautiful

00:46:52.780 --> 00:46:54.400
about the energy that's created

00:46:54.400 --> 00:46:56.500
every single time there's a performance,

00:46:56.500 --> 00:46:58.490
because it's such a safe
and welcoming space.

00:46:58.490 --> 00:47:00.290
And I'll really miss that.

00:47:01.300 --> 00:47:03.883
(upbeat music)

00:48:06.090 --> 00:48:08.920
- Think back on your
past four years at Yale.

00:48:08.920 --> 00:48:11.570
Did they turn out the way you expected?

00:48:11.570 --> 00:48:15.159
How has Yale changed you and
how have you changed Yale,

00:48:15.159 --> 00:48:17.320
and are you proud of what you've made

00:48:17.320 --> 00:48:18.543
of your four years here?

00:48:19.690 --> 00:48:21.150
As you listen to the final speech

00:48:21.150 --> 00:48:23.090
of this year's Class Day program,

00:48:23.090 --> 00:48:24.760
I encourage you to consider and reflect

00:48:24.760 --> 00:48:25.800
on your own college years

00:48:25.800 --> 00:48:28.930
and what it means for you to be a Yalie.

00:48:28.930 --> 00:48:31.320
Without further ado, I'm beyond
excited to present to you

00:48:31.320 --> 00:48:34.350
this year's serious reflection,
given by Alec Zbornak,

00:48:34.350 --> 00:48:37.680
a humanities major, of Ezra Stiles College.

00:48:37.680 --> 00:48:39.330
- My first winter break at Yale,

00:48:39.330 --> 00:48:42.002
I flew to Hawaii with
my improv group on tour,

00:48:42.002 --> 00:48:44.530
and on our third day
as we were coming back

00:48:44.530 --> 00:48:46.582
from an adventure to a roaring waterfall,

00:48:46.582 --> 00:48:49.053
we happened to stumble upon a rainbow.

00:48:49.900 --> 00:48:52.013
Immediately, we pulled
over, got out of the car,

00:48:52.013 --> 00:48:55.783
and as if on cue all started
sprinting towards it,

00:48:55.783 --> 00:48:58.730
cheering and jumping and
throwing our fists in the air

00:48:58.730 --> 00:49:01.460
like we were crazy, we
ran towards what felt like

00:49:01.460 --> 00:49:04.328
the perfect ending to the perfect day.

00:49:04.328 --> 00:49:07.230
But as we tried getting closer and closer,

00:49:07.230 --> 00:49:09.580
the rainbow kept slipping
further away from us.

00:49:11.660 --> 00:49:14.150
Three winter breaks have come
and gone since that tour,

00:49:14.150 --> 00:49:16.563
but the memory feels
especially relevant now.

00:49:17.670 --> 00:49:19.750
Each of us has spent the past four years

00:49:19.750 --> 00:49:22.950
running towards our
beautiful Yale finales.

00:49:22.950 --> 00:49:24.790
Every 1 a.m. paper in Bass,

00:49:24.790 --> 00:49:26.840
every late night grilled
cheese in the Buttery,

00:49:26.840 --> 00:49:29.710
every performance, every game, every test

00:49:29.710 --> 00:49:32.870
has been another step closer
to our senior springs,

00:49:32.870 --> 00:49:35.630
to a colorful time that's
supposed to be saturated

00:49:35.630 --> 00:49:38.709
with friends and parties and graduation.

00:49:38.709 --> 00:49:40.640
And then came the pandemic,

00:49:40.640 --> 00:49:43.750
and a lot of things changed about Yale.

00:49:43.750 --> 00:49:46.260
No more in-person
classes, no Harvard-Yale,

00:49:46.260 --> 00:49:49.188
no Masquerade, no Spring Fling.

00:49:49.188 --> 00:49:52.370
It's hard not to focus on all the loss.

00:49:52.370 --> 00:49:53.920
It's hard not to feel
like just when we were

00:49:53.920 --> 00:49:57.523
finally going to reach our
rainbow, it slipped away.

00:49:59.227 --> 00:50:01.340
But the more I think about it,

00:50:01.340 --> 00:50:03.590
the more I realize it's
always been this way.

00:50:04.470 --> 00:50:05.580
Even before the pandemic,

00:50:05.580 --> 00:50:07.757
from the moment we first
set foot on campus,

00:50:07.757 --> 00:50:09.220
there's always been things

00:50:09.220 --> 00:50:11.883
that we wanted to do but couldn't.

00:50:12.900 --> 00:50:15.417
In fact, there are whole
boards filled with them.

00:50:15.417 --> 00:50:17.260
You can't go more than 100 feet

00:50:17.260 --> 00:50:18.410
without running into one of these

00:50:18.410 --> 00:50:21.300
giant rectangular
bulletin boards on campus.

00:50:21.300 --> 00:50:23.625
Inside, outside, Old Campus, Cross Campus,

00:50:23.625 --> 00:50:27.900
even behind Toad's, and
each is filled to the brim

00:50:27.900 --> 00:50:30.673
with an array of glistening
flyers and posters.

00:50:31.730 --> 00:50:33.030
In our first years, my friends and I

00:50:33.030 --> 00:50:35.580
would stand in front of those
boards forever taking photos

00:50:35.580 --> 00:50:37.940
of all the things that we wanted to do.

00:50:37.940 --> 00:50:40.580
Improv-A-Ganza, Treasures
from the Yale Film Archive,

00:50:40.580 --> 00:50:42.790
Talk Like A Pirate Day,
Wilderness First Aid Course,

00:50:42.790 --> 00:50:43.733
Learn to Curl.

00:50:44.730 --> 00:50:46.460
It was a mosaic of opportunities.

00:50:46.460 --> 00:50:48.601
And just when we thought
that we'd done them all,

00:50:48.601 --> 00:50:51.700
someone would come and
staple a fresh batch.

00:50:51.700 --> 00:50:53.430
Civil Disobedience 101,

00:50:53.430 --> 00:50:55.290
Yale Photo Society Fall Exhibition,

00:50:55.290 --> 00:50:56.800
Perform with Yale Children's Theater,

00:50:56.800 --> 00:50:58.900
Conversations with Vice President Al Gore,

00:50:58.900 --> 00:51:00.970
the Silliman Haunted House is back!

00:51:00.970 --> 00:51:04.760
Our camera rolls quickly turned
into a Technicolor patchwork

00:51:04.760 --> 00:51:07.053
of club meetings and
events and performances.

00:51:08.250 --> 00:51:12.931
But as we soon realized, we
just couldn't do them all.

00:51:12.931 --> 00:51:16.540
Even this year with
everything that's changed,

00:51:16.540 --> 00:51:19.487
there's still so many things
that we can't do here.

00:51:19.487 --> 00:51:21.077
Things that we want to do,

00:51:21.077 --> 00:51:24.370
things that we wish we had the time to do,

00:51:24.370 --> 00:51:27.324
but things that we simply can't do.

00:51:27.324 --> 00:51:29.920
The performance of our
friend's one-person show

00:51:29.920 --> 00:51:32.800
interferes with the Ezra
Stiles hike to East Rock.

00:51:32.800 --> 00:51:36.520
The YSO concert with the CCAM
virtual reality workshop.

00:51:36.520 --> 00:51:39.430
The IM spikeball tournament
with the fall foliage tour.

00:51:39.430 --> 00:51:41.670
Do we want free cannolis in the courtyard

00:51:41.670 --> 00:51:44.210
or free chicken nuggets on Cross Campus?

00:51:44.210 --> 00:51:46.090
Do we attend the virtual college tea

00:51:46.090 --> 00:51:48.639
with the creator of
"Avatar: The Last Airbender"

00:51:48.639 --> 00:51:50.423
or with the Nobel Laureate?

00:51:51.450 --> 00:51:54.180
Even now there's so much to
do, so many people to meet,

00:51:54.180 --> 00:51:55.763
and friendships to solidify.

00:51:57.830 --> 00:52:00.763
I still think about that
rainbow in Hawaii a lot though.

00:52:01.640 --> 00:52:04.023
There's just something so Yale about it.

00:52:05.030 --> 00:52:07.160
From the time that we were
wide-eyed first years,

00:52:07.160 --> 00:52:09.960
we've been chasing the
perfect college experience

00:52:09.960 --> 00:52:11.330
where we do everything,

00:52:11.330 --> 00:52:15.500
take advantage of every
opportunity that Yale has to offer.

00:52:15.500 --> 00:52:16.980
Where by our senior springs,

00:52:16.980 --> 00:52:19.170
we could be editor-in-chief of the YDN,

00:52:19.170 --> 00:52:21.730
president of the YCC,
first chair of the YSO,

00:52:21.730 --> 00:52:23.320
and quarterback of the football team

00:52:23.320 --> 00:52:25.880
with four Ivy League
championship rings on our finger

00:52:25.880 --> 00:52:28.170
and a diploma reading
"Ultra Mega Summa Cum Laude"

00:52:28.170 --> 00:52:29.700
in our hands.

00:52:29.700 --> 00:52:31.910
Where we've gone on every
study abroad fellowship,

00:52:31.910 --> 00:52:34.310
watched and performed in every show,

00:52:34.310 --> 00:52:36.840
and met and become best
friends with everyone.

00:52:36.840 --> 00:52:39.590
We've checked off every flyer
from that bulletin board.

00:52:41.460 --> 00:52:43.390
But that's impossible.

00:52:43.390 --> 00:52:45.393
It's impossible to catch the rainbow,

00:52:46.934 --> 00:52:50.200
though if we've learned
anything from this year,

00:52:50.200 --> 00:52:53.393
it's that Yale isn't actually
about catching the rainbow.

00:52:54.690 --> 00:52:55.840
Yale's about realizing,

00:52:55.840 --> 00:52:59.010
even if it takes four
years and maybe a pandemic,

00:52:59.010 --> 00:53:01.973
that you can never catch
it, and that that's okay.

00:53:03.420 --> 00:53:06.510
Yale's about discovering
how you want to chase it.

00:53:06.510 --> 00:53:08.650
And it's about running
and jumping and laughing

00:53:08.650 --> 00:53:10.650
with the people you love the entire way,

00:53:11.580 --> 00:53:14.050
because it's lovely to chase rainbows,

00:53:14.050 --> 00:53:15.857
especially when the chase is as beautiful

00:53:15.857 --> 00:53:18.993
and vibrant and special as it was at Yale.

00:53:24.010 --> 00:53:26.530
- It can feel a little
cliche to talk about

00:53:26.530 --> 00:53:29.230
the importance of community at Yale,

00:53:29.230 --> 00:53:31.960
but the strength of those
relationships really came through

00:53:31.960 --> 00:53:34.220
in the first few months of the pandemic

00:53:34.220 --> 00:53:36.020
when we were so suddenly dispersed

00:53:36.020 --> 00:53:38.760
and yet relied on each other anyway.

00:53:38.760 --> 00:53:40.580
And it was those types of moments,

00:53:40.580 --> 00:53:43.120
like playing late night
"FIFA" games with suitemates,

00:53:43.120 --> 00:53:46.500
going on friends' tours of the YUAG,

00:53:46.500 --> 00:53:48.770
or celebrating blowout
wins against Harvard

00:53:48.770 --> 00:53:50.410
on the sports fields

00:53:50.410 --> 00:53:52.360
which build the types of relationships

00:53:52.360 --> 00:53:54.350
that make Yale so special

00:53:54.350 --> 00:53:57.831
and that I know can last a lifetime.

00:53:57.831 --> 00:54:00.498
(upbeat music)

00:55:36.308 --> 00:55:38.210
- Hello, Class of 2021.

00:55:38.210 --> 00:55:40.110
And on behalf of the Class Day Committee,

00:55:40.110 --> 00:55:42.830
we hope you've enjoyed this
virtual version of Class Day

00:55:42.830 --> 00:55:45.060
and that you've been able
to experience the feelings

00:55:45.060 --> 00:55:47.120
of camaraderie and joy that are

00:55:47.120 --> 00:55:49.140
at the heart of the celebration.

00:55:49.140 --> 00:55:51.460
A lot has changed from our
first year to our last,

00:55:51.460 --> 00:55:52.850
and I don't just mean the pandemic

00:55:52.850 --> 00:55:54.630
that has tried to define our senior year.

00:55:54.630 --> 00:55:56.100
in one way or another.

00:55:56.100 --> 00:55:58.350
Our class has been the
first to do many things.

00:55:58.350 --> 00:56:00.560
The first to live in the
brand-new residential colleges

00:56:00.560 --> 00:56:02.130
that soon became home.

00:56:02.130 --> 00:56:04.040
The first to experience the best YSO

00:56:04.040 --> 00:56:05.730
Halloween Show ever produced,

00:56:05.730 --> 00:56:08.010
and the first to see Marvin Chun's grades.

00:56:08.010 --> 00:56:11.355
The Class of 2021 is a group
of incredible game-changers,

00:56:11.355 --> 00:56:14.150
first leapers, and modernizers.

00:56:14.150 --> 00:56:15.770
It's my favorite thing about our class

00:56:15.770 --> 00:56:18.610
along with just how incredibly
passionate, strange,

00:56:18.610 --> 00:56:20.574
and unique we all are.

00:56:20.574 --> 00:56:23.510
And this Class Day is another
example of how we get creative

00:56:23.510 --> 00:56:26.050
and incite change in whatever we touch.

00:56:26.050 --> 00:56:28.100
The traditions Akhil talked
about at the beginning

00:56:28.100 --> 00:56:31.280
are still present on this day,
but we've made them our own.

00:56:31.280 --> 00:56:34.390
The ivy seeds you received
are part of a long tradition,

00:56:34.390 --> 00:56:37.440
but this year you can take
them where ever you go next.

00:56:37.440 --> 00:56:39.350
Plant them in your new homes and hopefully

00:56:39.350 --> 00:56:42.310
they'll remind you of the
incredible last four years.

00:56:42.310 --> 00:56:45.530
The anthology that we put
together also changed drastically,

00:56:45.530 --> 00:56:48.107
expanding to include
different forms of expression

00:56:48.107 --> 00:56:50.290
and hopefully encompassing as many parts

00:56:50.290 --> 00:56:52.408
of our Yale experience as possible.

00:56:52.408 --> 00:56:54.760
One thing that hasn't changed, though,

00:56:54.760 --> 00:56:57.260
is the tradition of hearing
"Bright College Years"

00:56:57.260 --> 00:56:59.830
at the beginning and
end of our time at Yale

00:56:59.830 --> 00:57:01.550
and waving the handkerchief you received

00:57:01.550 --> 00:57:04.390
either yesterday or even four years ago.

00:57:04.390 --> 00:57:07.240
It's a heartwarming tradition
that allowed us to say hello

00:57:07.240 --> 00:57:08.423
and now to say goodbye.

00:57:09.390 --> 00:57:12.590
Therefore it's my honor to
introduce the Yale Glee Club

00:57:12.590 --> 00:57:15.290
in a brand-new performance
of "Bright College Years."

00:57:15.290 --> 00:57:19.150
And lastly, from all of us,
congratulations, Class of 2021.

00:57:21.455 --> 00:57:26.455
♪ Bright College years,
with pleasure rife ♪

00:57:26.721 --> 00:57:31.721
♪ The shortest, gladdest years of life ♪

00:57:32.495 --> 00:57:37.495
♪ How swiftly are ye gliding by ♪

00:57:37.883 --> 00:57:42.883
♪ Oh, why doth time so quickly fly ♪

00:57:43.844 --> 00:57:48.844
♪ The seasons come, the seasons go ♪

00:57:49.475 --> 00:57:54.475
♪ The earth is green or white with snow ♪

00:57:56.145 --> 00:58:00.895
♪ But time and change shall naught avail ♪

00:58:07.799 --> 00:58:12.632
♪ To break the friendships
formed at Yale ♪

00:58:18.310 --> 00:58:23.310
♪ In after years, should troubles rise ♪

00:58:23.925 --> 00:58:28.925
♪ To cloud the blue of sunny skies ♪

00:58:29.822 --> 00:58:34.822
♪ How bright will seem,
through mem'ry's haze ♪

00:58:35.458 --> 00:58:40.458
♪ Those happy, golden, by-gone days ♪

00:58:41.410 --> 00:58:46.410
♪ Oh, let us strive that ever we ♪

00:58:47.195 --> 00:58:52.195
♪ May let these words our watch cry be ♪

00:58:53.730 --> 00:58:57.980
♪ Where'er upon life's sea we sail ♪

00:59:05.259 --> 00:59:09.592
♪ For God, for Country and for Yale ♪

00:59:16.880 --> 00:59:20.349
(crowd cheering)

00:59:20.349 --> 00:59:23.182
(energetic music)

01:02:29.084 --> 01:02:31.667
(upbeat music)

01:07:00.893 --> 01:07:03.476
(lively music)

